  "d2_vs_d3_contrast": "Epochs 1-3 (D2, header band 1.103-1.650 um) vs epochs 4-5 (D3, 1.630-2.435 um): the comet's aperture flux and the stars' fluxes are listed per epoch above; cross-detector comparisons mix bandpass change with time variability and must not be read as pure fading/brightening. Within-D2 epochs 1-3 and within-D3 epochs 4-5 are directly comparable.",
  "photometry_method_note": "Aperture SNR = excess / (annulus MAD-std * sqrt(N_ap)). NOTE: images were reprojected (reproject_interp), which correlates neighbouring-pixel noise, so formal aperture SNRs are optimistic upper estimates; they reproduce Phase 1's independent checks for the comet (~92-117) and are given for relative comparison. Bright-star SNRs in the thousands reflect the very smooth local background of the reprojected images, not photon statistics.",
  "comet_flux_trend_note": "Comet aperture flux (MJy/sr*px): 66.8, 66.4, 66.1 (D2, epochs 1-3, flat within ~1%), then 55.9, 46.0 (D3, epochs 4-5). The D2->D3 step mixes bandpass change with any real fading and must not be quoted as pure fading. Simultaneously the LVF effective wavelength sampled at the comet rose monotonically 1.213 -> 1.827 um across the 5 epochs because the comet moved across the detectors' wavelength gradient.",
